summary:
I've often heard "I wish PyTorch had more dev internals documentation" when in reality the problem is we have too much. PyTorch is a deep project and it touches on pretty much all aspects of computer science so here are my favorite references
Intro
@tarantulae for an overview of everything
@shshnkp for an overview of torch.compile
@soumithchintala blog on the PyTorch philosophy and design principles
The PyTorch blog tends on more accessible and immediately useful stuff that's being shipped
Intermediate
The PyTorch docs we have some basic torch.compile internals in but please complain if you feel something is still hard to grok
For the YouTube inclined we had a series of interviews with many of the core developers in this 2.0 series
The most polished talks tend to be at the PyTorch conference every year
After skimming all of the above you should be able to confidently talk about various pieces of PyTorch but if you wanna contribute to some complex part of the stack and be aware of the nightly changes then
@Zachary_DeVito frequently posts about CUDA memory management
@cHHillee on compilers - he's looking for new content suggestions!
@ezyang PyTorch dev podcast has just restarted - Ed is the GOAT you can learn a lot listening to him. His blog is also fantastic
If you ever wanna learn more about torch.compile internals, why tracing FSDP is hard, what is FSDP even, what's going with our float8 support most of the updates get shared on our dev blog
I've been working on PyTorch full time for close to 3 years and I still find many parts of it hard to grok so take your time and enjoy, regardless of which random blogs or posts you'll read from the above, I can promise you will learn a lot

