Muon is not Kimi's optimizer. It was first invented by Keller Jordan of OpenAI to speed up Andrej Karpathy's nanoGPT (now nanochat). Kimi was the first to verify that "Muon is Scalable for LLM Training" and adopt it in their models.

@jenzhuscott: "Kimi k2.6 used DeepSeek’s v3 architecture. DeepSeek v4 used Kimi's Muon optimizer. 1.6 trillion parameters and 1M context—both match or beat closed models on benchmarks while being 8x cheaper. Both build on each other's breakthroughs. Both keep shipping frontier LLMs with far less and nerfed NVIDIA GPUs. China’s full domestic stack/ecosystem chip-model-cloud is now on full speed. Debate on distillation all you want. The dragon is fully awake and good luck stopping it."

Muon is an optimizer for the hidden layers in neural networks, achieving current training speed records for both NanoGPT and CIFAR-10 speedrunning.

Welcome, AlphaChip! Today, we are sharing exciting updates on our work published in @Nature in 2021, which uses reinforcement learning for ASIC chip floorplanning and layout. Since its publication, AlphaChip has been utilized for multiple generations of TPU chips, achieving significant improvements in block placement and wirelength reductions compared to human experts.

We are also releasing a pre-trained AlphaChip checkpoint to facilitate external users in their chip designs. This model has been pre-trained on 20 TPU blocks, enabling good zero-shot performance and faster convergence for novel blocks. Many organizations, including MediaTek, have successfully used AlphaChip for their advanced chip designs, enhancing power, performance, and chip area.

@GoogleDeepMind: "Our AI for chip design method AlphaChip has transformed the way we design microchips. From helping to design state-of-the-art TPUs for building AI models to CPUs in data centers, its widespread impact can be seen across Alphabet and beyond."

